speaker 1: What exactly does bare metal mean, and how's it different from a regular VM?
speaker 2: A regular VM runs a Hypervisor layer on the physical machine and slices it into many VMs for different customers. Bare metal hands you the whole physical machine directly, with no virtualization layer in between.
speaker 1: On AWS side, that's m5.metal-type instances, right?
speaker 2: Right, AWS Bare Metal instances follow this same idea - the concept maps completely to OCI's version.
speaker 1: So what scenarios call for bare metal instead of a regular VM?
speaker 2: One typical scenario is a database extremely sensitive to performance that can't tolerate any virtualization overhead. Another is when you need to install your own Hypervisor layer, like running VMware.
speaker 1: What does "installing your own Hypervisor" mean - can't you do that on a regular VM?
speaker 2: A regular VM already has one virtualization layer, so you can't nest a complete virtualization platform on top of it. Bare metal is a physical machine, so you're free to install your own virtualization software.
speaker 1: How committed is OCI to bare metal?
speaker 2: According to official materials, OCI invested in bare metal very early on, and the machine type selection is fairly rich - unlike other cloud vendors' early "VM-first" strategy.
speaker 1: So the decision logic is: use Flex VM for general scenarios, and only go bare metal for extreme performance needs or when you need to install a Hypervisor?
speaker 2: Right, that's the same decision logic as choosing between a regular EC2 instance and a Bare Metal instance on AWS.
